  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1247 AM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nFriday: For the end of the week, another brief period of active \nweather is expected for south central and southeastern Colorado. A \nbroad and open wave will again push southeastward over the region \nthrough Friday. While forcing won't be overly strong with this wave, \nsupport from it, and a slight uptick in orographics, will help to \nsupport isolated to scattered shower develop along the mountains \nthrough the day. Snow levels will hover around 8,700ft, with snow \nabove this and mostly rain below it. Any snow that falls along the \nhigher terrain will be fairly minimal, around 1-1.5 inches or less. \nWith that all said, any showers present are expected to dissipate \novernight Friday as the wave weakens and pushes further to \nsoutheast. Beyond all of that, skies will be partly to mostly cloudy \nthrough much of the day, with mostly clear skies returning by \nSaturday morning. Temperatures for Friday will warm just a tad, with \nmuch of the area hovering around near seasonal values.\n\nSaturday - Wednesday: For the rest of the long term period, quiet \nweather makes a return to south central and southeastern Colorado. \nOverall, ridging will build across the region, bringing increased \ndescent and drier air. With this pattern in place, dry conditions \nare anticipated for the region. While there are some hints of weak \nwaves being embedded within the flow, confidence remains very low \n(10-20%) on any influence from these features at this time. Outside \nof that, mostly clear to partly cloudy skies with relatively light \nwinds can be expected. Looking at temperatures, a warming trend is \nanticipated, with much of the area warming to above seasonal\nvalues.",
